  "query_preview": "..\n\nPartly to mostly cloudy skies continue across most of southern\nArizona with the exception of Cochise county. Isolated storms\nand showers are beginning to form along the International\nborder and in far northern Greenlee county in the White\nMountains. Moisture continues to surge into southern Arizona \nwith satellite derived PWATs now 1.4 to 1.75 inches as far east\nas central Cochise and Graham counties. Storm coverage this\nafternoon into the evening will be focused to locales along the\nInternational Border, including Santa Cruz and Cochise county,\nand the AZ/NM border. Like the past few days, the main concern \nwill be locally heavy rainfall that could lead to localized \nflash flooding. \n\nThe deep trough extending from the Pacific Northwest will\nretreat northward today as high pressure from the Central US \npushes westward. High pressure will continue to build throughout\nthe week in the Southwest US as storm activity and coverage \nwill slowly. Currently,the more active days looks to be \nWednesday and Thursday as the area of high pressure moves \neastward overhead, introducing more daytime heating and \ninstability, as well as easterly to northeasterly flow aloft. \nThe available moisture continues to have flash flooding as the \nprimary threat, however stronger storms during this time can be \ncapable of strong outflow winds as well.\n\nTemperatures will be within a couple of degrees of normal through \nThursday, then dropping to below normal levels through the\nweekend into early next week.",
